# Context

## Background
T070's workload matrix (docs/performance.md, workload 4) found that a joined
device with an established sync connection never re-uploaded after the hub
process restarted: dials failed at the 60 s deadline in a backoff loop for
at least three minutes, 5/5 reproductions. A fresh node connected to the
restarted hub in about 20 s, and restarting only the join process reconnected
in about 25 s — so the stale state lived in the long-lived device node, not
in the hub or the sync layer.

## Why This Way
Live diagnosis during the failure window showed the device node's control
connections to the coordination plane dead in CLOSE-WAIT with no re-poll
ever issued: when the plane restarts inside the hub process, the tsnet
control client in tailscale.com v1.104.4 never recovers on its own, and the
cached peer endpoints stop matching the restarted hub. LocalAPI debug
actions (force-netmap-update) only replay the cached netmap, so the only
reliable recovery is a node rebuild from the same durable identity — the
observed-good path. The rebuild triggers only when the coordination plane
still answers an HTTP request, which distinguishes "hub restarted, node
stale" from "hub down" (the plane and the hub live in one process), and the
20 s internal dial deadline leaves enough of the uploader's 60 s budget for
the rebuild to complete inside a single dial attempt.

## Gotchas
- A borrower never rebuilds the owner's node; an idle owner holding the lock
  while wedged is documented as a limit in ADR 0006.
- The uploader may still spend up to one 60 s exchange timeout on a
  silently-dead connection before the first failing dial; recovery measured
  30.7-31.2 s per restart cycle (36.2 s in the full workload-4 run).
- The intermittent hub-side "panic: ipnlocal: watchdog timeout" (1 of 3
  manual restarts during T070) did not recur in 10 post-fix restart cycles;
  it is an upstream deadlock probe and stays monitored (NOTES.md).

